Title: "Via Keyword"
Id: "0ec4066a-bb89-469f-bdf3-7958dd871eda"

Text: "`via` records the view used by an extension.

```mlg-fragment
G is \set via X
```

The left side is an `is` statement. The right side is a form or declaration —
such as `X` or `(X, Y)` — describing how the subtype is viewed as the supertype.

In a definition, `extends: G is \set via X` lets the checker use `G is \set`
whenever it knows `G` has the described type. The implication is recursive, so
subtype chains are followed.

The `via` form also **sets the types of the symbols it names**, so they need not
be repeated in `declares:`:

- `extends: M is \set via X` — a single `via` symbol becomes an instance of the
  extended type, recording `X is \set`.
- `extends: S is \magma via (X, *)` — a `via` tuple maps positionally onto the
  extended type's own components, so `X` and `*` inherit the types `\magma`
  gives its components (`X is \set`, `* is \binary.operation:on{S}`).

Because `via` supplies those types, `declares:` only needs to type the
components `via` does not cover.

An `extends:` section may also use a spec statement, such as
`extends: x \"in\" X`, which records the corresponding spec fact as an
implication for values of the owning type."
Id: "84a2b89a-dc30-43e5-8648-2bf8f5b368f5"
